-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/50a6cbab-58ed-52c5-9a58-8c56ce4ca163
-- title:
--   Hecke modules on a cube-free ladder and at Taylor–Wiles levels
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $\mathcal O$ is a characteristic-zero complete discrete valuation ring with finite residue field $k = \mathrm{ResidueField}\,\mathcal O$, and $p$ is an odd prime with $p \in \mathfrak m_{\mathcal O}$ (hypotheses `hp2`, `hp𝒪`).
--
--   Residual data. $\bar\rho$ is a residual Galois representation over $k$: a two-dimensional $k$-vector space $\bar\rho.V$ with a monoid homomorphism from $\mathrm{Aut}_{\mathbb Q}(\overline{\mathbb Q})$ to $\mathrm{End}_k(\bar\rho.V)$ which is trivial on the subgroup fixing some finite subextension of $\mathbb Q$ in $\overline{\mathbb Q}$; `habs` asserts that its base change to $\overline k$ has no invariant subspace other than $\bot$ and $\top$. Finite sets of naturals $S \supseteq S_{\min}$ are given with all elements of $S$ prime (`hS`), $p \in S_{\min}$ (`hpSmin`, `hSmin`), and `hmin`: for every prime $q \neq p$, $q \in S_{\min}$ if and only if $\bar\rho$ is ramified at $q$, i.e. some valuation subring $P$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $q \in P^{\mathrm{nonunits}}$ has an element of its inertia subgroup acting non-trivially. The hypothesis `htame` asserts that for every $q \in S_{\min}$ with $q \neq p$, the representation $\bar\rho$ viewed as an adic representation over $k$ is unipotent on inertia at $q$: for every $P$ lying over $q$ and every $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup of $P$, the characteristic polynomial of $\bar\rho(\sigma)$ is $(X-1)^2$.
--
--   The ladder of levels. A natural number $n$, a sequence of nonzero levels $N_\bullet =$ `Nf`, and a sequence of primes $q_\bullet =$ `qf` are given, subject to: `hNS`, every prime divisor of every $N_k$ lies in $S$; `hNp`, for $k \le n$, $p^2 \nmid N_k$ and $p \mid N_k \iff p \mid N_n$; `hNmin`, for $k \le n$ every $q \in S_{\min}$ with $q \neq p$ divides $N_k$; `hNunr`, for $k \le n$ every prime $q \neq p$ outside $S_{\min}$ dividing $N_k$ divides it to order at least two; `hN3`, for $k \le n$ no prime $q \neq p$ has $q^3 \mid N_k$; `hfoot`, $N_n$ is squarefree and its prime divisors other than $p$ all lie in $S_{\min}$; and `hrung`, for each $k < n$ the number $q_k$ is a prime in $S$ distinct from $p$ such that one of the three alternatives holds: $q_k \nmid N_{k+1}$ and $N_k = N_{k+1}q_k^2$; or $q_k \| N_{k+1}$ and $N_k = N_{k+1}q_k$; or $q_k^2 \mid N_{k+1}$ and $N_k = N_{k+1}q_k$.
--
--   Ihara input at the rungs (`hihara`). For each $k < n$ with $q_k^2 \nmid N_{k+1}$, for every $\mathcal O$-module $A$, every nonzero $\ell_0$ and every $M$, given level comparison data [`CohCarrier.LevelLE`](def/CohCarrier_Level.html#L330) for the pairs $(M, Mq_k)$ and $(Mq_k, Mq_k^2)$ with full character subgroups and degeneracy parameters $1$ and $q_k$, and assuming $q_k \nmid M$, that multiplication by $q_k$ is injective on $A$, that $\ell_0$ is prime and $\ell_0 \nmid Mq_k$: first, whenever $g, h$ in $H^1(M) = \mathrm{Hom}(\Gamma_H(M)^{\mathrm{add}}, A)$ satisfy $i_1 g + i_{q_k} h = 0$ in $H^1(Mq_k)$, both $g$ and $h$ are Eisenstein at $\ell_0$ in the sense that $T_{\ell_0}$ acts on them as $\ell_0 + 1$; second, whenever $x, z'$ in $H^1(Mq_k)$ satisfy $i_1 x + i_{q_k} z' = 0$ in $H^1(Mq_k^2)$, there is $w \in H^1(M)$ with $z' - i_1 w$ and $x + i_{q_k} w$ both Eisenstein at $\ell_0$ in this sense.
--
--   The auxiliary prime. A prime $r$ is given with $r \ge 5$ (`hr5`), $r \notin S$ (`hrS`), $r \nmid N_k p$ for all $k \le n$ (`hrN`), $p \nmid r-1$ (`hr1`), and `hrρ`: for every valuation subring $P$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ lying over $r$ and every $\sigma$ which is a Frobenius at $r$ for $P$ (acting as $x \mapsto x^r$ on the residue field), $\mathrm{tr}\,\bar\rho(\sigma)^2 \neq (r+1)^2$ in $k$. Each level $N_k$ in weight $2$ is assumed to have an integral structure, in the sense that the $\mathbb Z$-span of the cusp forms with integral $q$-expansion coefficients spans the full $\mathbb C$-vector space.
--
--   Hecke data. For each $k$, $\theta_k =$ `θf k` is a ring homomorphism from the Hecke algebra $\mathbb T(N_k, 2, S)$ — the $\mathbb Z$-subalgebra of $\mathrm{End}_{\mathbb C}(S_2(\Gamma_0(N_k)))$ generated by the $T_\ell$ for primes $\ell \nmid N_k$, $\ell \notin S$, and the $U_q$ for primes $q \mid N_k$, $q \notin S$ — to $k$; `hθ` asserts that $\theta_k$ computes the Frobenius characteristic polynomials of $\bar\rho$: for every prime $\ell$ with $\ell \nmid N_k$ and $\ell \notin S$, every $P$ over $\ell$ and every Frobenius $\sigma$ at $\ell$ for $P$, the characteristic polynomial of $\bar\rho(\sigma)$ is $X^2 - \theta_k(T_\ell)X + \ell$. A homomorphism $\chi$ from $\mathbb T(N_n,2,S)$ to $\mathcal O$ is given lifting $\theta_n$ (`hχ`). For each $k$, $\pi T_k$ is an $\mathcal O$-algebra map from the localisation $\mathbb T_{\theta_k}$ ([`CuspForm.heckeLocal`](def/CuspForm_HeckeLocal.html#L131), the localisation of the base-changed Hecke algebra at the complement of the kernel of the character attached to $\theta_k$) to $\mathcal O$, and `hπT` asserts compatibility with $\chi$: for $k \le n$ and every prime $\ell \notin S$ dividing neither $N_k$ nor $N_n$, $\pi T_k(\pi(T_\ell)) = \chi(T_\ell)$.
--
--   Level-raising traces. A sequence $a_\bullet$ in $\mathcal O$ is given together with `ha`: for each $k < n$ with $q_k \nmid N_{k+1}$ there is an adic Galois representation $\rho'$ over $\mathcal O$ (a free $\mathcal O$-module of rank $2$ with an adically continuous action) whose Frobenius characteristic polynomial at every prime $\ell \nmid N_{k+1}$, $\ell \notin S$, is $X^2 - \pi T_{k+1}(\pi(T_\ell))X + \ell$, which is unramified at $q_k$, and whose trace at any Frobenius at $q_k$ equals $a_k$.
--
--   Finally, $H_n$ is a [`CuspForm.HeckeGaloisRepDatum`](def/CuspForm_HeckeGaloisRepDatum.html#L8) for level $N_n$, weight $2$, set $S$, ring $\mathcal O$, character $\theta_n$ and Hecke ring $\mathbb T_{\theta_n}$ itself: a homomorphism $\pi$ from $\mathbb T(N_n,2,S)$ to $\mathbb T_{\theta_n}$ reducing to $\theta_n$, with $\mathcal O$-algebra generated by its image, such that every lift $\chi$ of $\theta_n$ to $\mathcal O$ factors through $\pi$, together with an adic representation $\rho$ over $\mathbb T_{\theta_n}$ whose Frobenius characteristic polynomials are $X^2 - \pi(T_\ell)X + \ell$ and whose residual representation is absolutely irreducible.
--
--   Conclusion. There exist a family of types $M_k$, each an abelian group carrying a $\mathbb T_{\theta_k}$-module structure and an $\mathcal O$-module structure forming a scalar tower, finite and free over $\mathcal O$, together with $\mathcal O$-bilinear pairings $B_k \colon M_k \times M_k \to \mathcal O$, such that the following hold.
--
--   (1) For $k \le n$, each $B_k$ is Hecke-adjoint: $B_k(t\cdot m, m') = B_k(m, t\cdot m')$ for all $t \in \mathbb T_{\theta_k}$ and $m, m' \in M_k$.
--
--   (2) For $k \le n$, the map $m \mapsto B_k(m, \cdot)$ is bijective, i.e. $B_k$ is a perfect pairing.
--
--   (3) For $k \le n$, the submodule of $M_k$ annihilated by $\ker(\pi T_k)$ is nonzero.
--
--   (4) For $k \le n$, $\mathrm{rank}_{\mathcal O} M_k$ equals the product of the $\mathcal O$-rank of that $\ker(\pi T_k)$-torsion submodule and the $\mathcal O$-rank of $\mathbb T_{\theta_k}$.
--
--   (5) For each $k < n$ there are $\mathcal O$-linear maps $i \colon M_{k+1} \to M_k$ and $j \colon M_k \to M_{k+1}$ and an element $\Delta \in \mathbb T_{\theta_{k+1}}$ such that: $i$ and $j$ are adjoint, $B_{k+1}(j m', m) = B_k(m', i m)$; $j \circ i$ is multiplication by $\Delta$ on $M_{k+1}$; $i$ carries the $\ker(\pi T_{k+1})$-torsion submodule of $M_{k+1}$ onto the $\ker(\pi T_k)$-torsion submodule of $M_k$ (as $\mathcal O$-submodules); $\pi T_{k+1}(\Delta) \neq 0$; if $q_k \nmid N_{k+1}$ then $(q_k - 1)\bigl(a_k^2 - (q_k+1)^2\bigr)$ divides $\pi T_{k+1}(\Delta)$ in $\mathcal O$; and if $q_k \| N_{k+1}$ then $q_k^2 - 1$ divides $\pi T_{k+1}(\Delta)$.
--
--   (6) Taylor–Wiles modules, strict-ordinary case. Assume $p \mid N_n$ and that $\bar\rho$, viewed as an adic representation over $k$, is not flat at $p$. Then for every $t$, every injective $qv \colon \mathrm{Fin}\,t \to \mathbb N$ with each $qv_i$ prime, $qv_i \notin S$ and $p \mid qv_i - 1$, with each $qv_i \ge 5$ and with $\bar\rho$ having distinct Frobenius eigenvalues at each $qv_i$ (for every $P$ over $qv_i$ and every Frobenius $\varphi$ at $qv_i$ there are $\alpha \neq \beta$ in $k$ with $\mathrm{charpoly}\,\bar\rho(\varphi) = (X-\alpha)(X-\beta)$), the following holds. Let $D_{\min}$ be a deformation ring datum for $\bar\rho$ and the minimal strict-ordinary condition at $p$ with ramification allowed in $S_{\min}$ (cyclotomic determinant, strict ordinarity at $p$, unramified outside $S_{\min}$, and unipotent on inertia at the $q \in S_{\min}$, $q \neq p$), let $D_Q$ be a deformation ring datum for the condition that is strict ordinary with ramification allowed in $S_{\min} \cup \mathrm{image}(qv)$ and unipotent on inertia at all $q \in S_{\min}$, $q \neq p$, let $\varphi \colon D_{\min}.R \to \mathbb T_{\theta_n}$ be a local $\mathcal O$-algebra map such that the base change of $D_{\min}.\rho$ along $\varphi$ is equivalent to $H_n.\rho$; let $\mathrm{cyc}$ assign to each $q$ a character of the absolute Galois group into $(\mathbb Z/q)^\times$, $\pi\Delta$ a homomorphism $(\mathbb Z/q)^\times \to \mathbb Z/p^{v_p(q-1)}$ (multiplicatively written), $\varepsilon \colon D_Q.R \to D_{\min}.R$ and $\iota$ an $\mathcal O$-algebra map from the monoid algebra $\mathcal O[\prod_i \mathbb Z/p^{v_p(qv_i-1)}]$ to $D_Q.R$, subject to: $\mathrm{cyc}\,q$ describes the Galois action on $q$-th roots of unity, $\pi\Delta\,q$ is surjective for every prime $q \neq p$, $\varepsilon$ is local and carries $D_Q.\rho$ to a representation equivalent to $D_{\min}.\rho$, $\varepsilon \circ \iota$ sends each group element to $1$, $\ker \varepsilon$ is generated by the elements $\iota(g) - 1$, and for each $i$ and each $P$ over $qv_i$ the representation $D_Q.\rho$ has a basis $b_0, b_1$ on which inertia at $P$ acts by the characters $\iota(\mathrm{mulSingle}_i\,\pi\Delta(\mathrm{cyc}\,\sigma))$ and its inverse. Then there exists a type $M_n'$ which is an abelian group, a module over $D_Q.R$, over $\Lambda := \mathcal O[\prod_i \mathbb Z/p^{v_p(qv_i-1)}]$ and over $\mathcal O$ in a scalar tower, such that $M_n'$ is nontrivial, finite over $\mathcal O$, free over $\Lambda$, the $\Lambda$-action agrees with the $D_Q.R$-action through $\iota$, and there is an $\mathcal O$-linear isomorphism $e$ from the quotient of $M_n'$ by the augmentation ideal of $\Lambda$ times $M_n'$ onto $M_n$ which is equivariant for $D_Q.R$ acting on the source and for $\mathbb T_{\theta_n}$ acting on the target through $\varphi \circ \varepsilon$: $e(\overline{x \cdot m}) = \varphi(\varepsilon x)\cdot e(\bar m)$.
--
--   (7) Taylor–Wiles modules, flat case. Assume $p \nmid N_n$. Then the same conclusion as in (6) holds verbatim, with the minimal strict-ordinary condition replaced by the minimal flat condition at $p$ with ramification in $S_{\min}$ for $D_{\min}$, and the strict-ordinary condition replaced by the flat condition with ramification in $S_{\min} \cup \mathrm{image}(qv)$ (together with unipotence on inertia at the $q \in S_{\min}$, $q \neq p$) for $D_Q$; here flatness at $p$ is the condition that the residue field is finite and every finite-quotient level of the representation arises from the $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-points of a finite flat commutative cocommutative Hopf algebra over $\mathbb Z$ localised away from $p$, Galois-equivariantly.
--
--   This is the cube-free-level version of the construction of the cohomological Hecke modules used in the Taylor–Wiles argument: it produces simultaneously a ladder of $\mathcal O$-free, self-dual Hecke modules $M_k$ along a chain of levels descending to the minimal level $N_n$, with congruence-module indices controlled by the level-raising factors $(q_k-1)(a_k^2-(q_k+1)^2)$ and $q_k^2-1$, and, at the bottom level, Taylor–Wiles modules over the group-ring $\Lambda$ attached to a set of auxiliary primes $qv_i$, free over $\Lambda$ and recovering $M_n$ on taking $\Lambda$-coinvariants. The result feeds the numerical-criterion step of the modularity lifting theorem and is cited by [`CuspForm.heckeLocal.exists_heckeModules_levelRaising_and_taylorWiles_of_index_two_irreducible_strictOrdinary_of_not_cube_dvd`](thm.html#CuspForm.heckeLocal.exists_heckeModules_levelRaising_and_taylorWiles_of_index_two_irreducible_strictOrdinary_of_not_cube_dvd); it is obtained from the corresponding level-ladder statement and the two auxiliary-level Taylor–Wiles module constructions, in the non-flat strict-ordinary and the flat cases respectively.
